  @media (min-width: 1440px) {
    .floor__scene {
      --fl-plate-size: 13px;
    }
  }

  /*
    DESIGN.md §C.6 — the fixed slot. The reservation is not a guess: 344 is derived in
    §C.6 from the longest card (Tanya's) at the >=1024 sizes in a 366px slot, and 320
    from the same arithmetic at 360's sizes. If a copy edit ever overflows it, the fix
    is the copy or the padding, never a shorter reservation.
  */
  /*
    Step-2 review item 4, and §J's pre-committed cut for `/`: the slot has NO
    container. It is a reservation, not a box — no border, no outline, no fill, no
    radius, no shadow. A 1px rule drawn round a 344px reservation turns invisible space
    into a visible empty container, and it was the only outlined rectangle on the site.
    The card's own internal rule above `Checked by` stays; that one carries meaning.
  */
  .floor__slot {
    min-block-size: var(--card-slot-min);
    padding-block: var(--space-24);
  }

  .station-card__lead,
  .station-card__body {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-8);
    color: var(--tgd-on-room-muted);
  }

  .station-card__gate {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-16);
    padding-block-start: var(--space-16);
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--rule-on-room);
  }

  .station-card__gate-label {
    font-size: var(--step-micro);
    line-height: var(--step-micro-lh);
    color: var(--tgd-on-room-muted);
  }

  .station-card__gate-name {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-4);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  /*
    §C.6's four reservations. Round 12 re-derives three of the four against MEASUREMENT
    rather than against arithmetic (review item H6, `measure-floor.json`): 320 below 768,
    **264** at 768-1023, **416** at 1024-1439 and **352** at 1440.

    The old numbers came out of Tanya's card, computed before design review and the
    release cut had owners; COPY §2.3 round 12 put both names on both lists and Sahib's
    gate line is now the long one. Measured with each card forced visible in the slot, the
    tallest card is 406.7 at 1024 against a 392 reservation — 15px SHORT of its own
    longest card — 339.2 at 1440 against 344, and 251.3 at 768 against 344, which is 93px
    of reserved void under a two-line card. Every number here is the measured maximum
    rounded up to the next 8px step with at least one step of slack, and §C.6's own rule
    is what it applies: the fix is the copy or the padding, never a shorter reservation —
    and reserving 93px of nothing is as much a defect as reserving 15px too few.
  */
  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .floor__slot {
      min-block-size: 264px;
    }
  }

  /*
    §C.6: "The side-by-side arrives at 1024, and it always did." The keep-out lane that
    used to hold it back to 1440 is withdrawn (step-2 review, ruling (b)), so the slot
    takes cols 9-12 at 1024 — 293px there, 384px at 1440 — vertically centred to the
    scene, and it never drops below the roster.
  */
  @media (min-width: 1024px) {
    .floor__body {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) var(--card-slot-width);
      /*
        §C.3: "The box's right edge is the card slot's left edge." No gutter is drawn
        between them — the room's optical air is the scene box's own transparent 76-unit
        right margin plus the slot's 24 of padding, which is 100px at 1440.
      */
      gap: 0;
      align-items: center;
    }

    /*
      §C.3: the box's left edge "is where the floor section's full bleed allows it to
      go, because what sits in that margin is empty scene and never a mark". That bleed
      is the section's own outer margin, so the box is its column plus that margin,
      capped at 856. At 1024 it comes out at 682 (610 + 24 + 48) and at 1440 and 1920 at
      the 856 cap, with the right edge on the slot in all three.
    */
    .floor__scene {
      justify-self: end;
      inline-size: min(856px, calc(100% + var(--outer)));
      margin-inline: 0;
    }

    .floor__slot {
      min-block-size: 416px;
      padding-inline: var(--space-24);
    }
  }

  @media (min-width: 1440px) {
